seg-22d0b06d5df4 = Every chart Kshana renders — in the browser playground, the CLI (command-line interface)'s <c1/> export, and the chart inside the printable <c2/> (HTML, HyperText Markup Language) report — is stamped, bottom-right, with:

# text
# c1: scenario <hash>
# c2: scenario_hash
# c3: scenario_hash
seg-d8b4de856202 = The <c1/> is the first { "12" } hex characters of the run's <b>scenario hash</b>: a SHA-256 (SHA: Secure Hash Algorithm) over the <em>canonical</em> scenario definition (seed, thresholds, model parameters, GNSS windows, and so on). It is the same fingerprint that appears in the one-line run summary and in the result JSON's <c2/> field. (Kinds whose result document carries no <c3/>, such as the integrity and lunar reports, fall back to a SHA-256 of the scenario source TOML (Tom's Obvious, Minimal Language), so every chart still has a stable fingerprint.)

# text
seg-8d218131da01 = Because the hash is deterministic and input-sensitive, a saved or pasted chart image is self-identifying: it records the engine version, the exact scenario that produced it (for bit-for-bit reproduction), and the source — and any altered parameter yields a different hash, so a mislabelled chart is detectable.

# text
seg-89c9b001868f = How to cite & reproduce

# text
# c1: scenario + seed + engine version
# c2: CITATION.cff
# c3: provenance
seg-6eca19b7978c = Every result is reproducible from <c1/>. Cite the engine via <a1><c2/></a1> / the Zenodo concept DOI <a2>10.5281/zenodo.20528627</a2>, and cite the <em>parameter</em> sources above for the figures a given run depends on — they travel in the result JSON's <c3/> fields, so a published result is self-documenting.

# text
seg-c5c58ef3771c = Where a figure is a space goal on ground hardware (every strontium optical-clock number) or a ground-lab demonstration (cold-atom accelerometer), this document and the result provenance say so explicitly. Kshana does not present lab or goal figures as flown performance.

